# The fixture corpus of tests/SharpCorePDF.Tests/Assets is read by byte and by offset: a document whose
# line endings were converted is a different document, because every entry of its cross-reference table
# points at an offset that moved. Two of the AES fixtures carry no byte Git detects as binary, so it
# treated them as text and rewrote them on checkout (1119 -> 1155 bytes). Declaring PDFs binary also
# keeps their diffs out of the text viewer, where they only ever read as mojibake.
*.pdf binary

# The same reason, for every file the parity manifest hashes. `manifest.json` records each fixture's
# SHA-256 and byte count, and Git's line-ending normalisation silently broke it: the labelled exports
# were generated with CRLF on Windows, committed as LF blobs, matched their manifest on the machine
# that made them and failed `ParityCorpusTests` on the Linux CI leg. The generator now writes LF
# explicitly; this keeps the checkout from converting anything back.
tests/SharpCorePDF.Tests/Assets/parity/** -text
